<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afanasy Nikitin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">?–c. 1475</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afanasy Nikitin was a merchant of Tver who travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between about 1466 and 1472, one of the first Europeans to describe the country. His travel notes, A Journey Beyond Three Seas, record life in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, where he stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>. He died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> on his way home, around 1475.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afanasy-nikitin.jpg" alt="Afanasy Nikitin" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexxx1979 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tver._Monument_to_Afanasy_Nikitin_P7221267_2350.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Afanasy Nikitin, son of Nikita, was a merchant in Tver. Before his great journey he had probably traded over long distances and had travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, Moldavia, Wallachia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. He left Tver on a commercial trip planning to trade around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> as far as Shirvan, and travelled down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a>, Plyos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>, where he joined the caravan of Hasan Beg, the returning ambassador from Shirvan.</p><p>Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> his party was robbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> and again by Kaitags on the Caspian coast, and some of his companions were taken prisoner. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> rescued them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shemakha</a> but would not give them the means to return home, so the merchants dispersed. Some went home, others stayed at Shemakha or worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hoping to recoup his losses, Nikitin went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and crossed the Caspian into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. He stayed six months at Chapakur and passed through Sari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a>, Kashan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a> to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, spending two years in Persia in all. Muslim merchants told him of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s riches, and he sailed from Hormuz, reaching the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaul">Chaul</a> six weeks later.</p><p>He observed the markets, ways of life and courts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, visited the sanctuary of Parvattum, which he called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, and dealt in horses. After almost three years he concluded that further travel would bring no great profit. He returned by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in November 1474 and probably wrote his narrative there. He died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in the spring of 1475. Other merchants took his notes to Vasily Mamyrev, secretary to Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and they entered state chronicles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Nikitin's narrative, A Journey Beyond the Three Seas, describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s population, political structure, agriculture, trade, customs and ceremonies. He noted that the countrymen were very poor while the boyars were rich, and he described the four months of rain he spent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a>. Historians have used it as a dependable source for 15th-century India, though some of his accounts of everyday life are exaggerated and he accepted legends such as that of a monkey prince with armed followers.</p><p>Because he transliterated Muslim prayers into Cyrillic and kept Muslim fasts, Gail Lenhoff and Janet Martin speculated that he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The editor Yakov Lurye doubts this, noting that a convert would hardly have tried to return to Rus', and calls his piety a peculiar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syncretism">syncretism</a>. Nikitin himself wrote of shedding tears for the Christian faith and longing to go home.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afanasy Nikitin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Russian merchant <b>Afanasy Nikitin</b> stayed in Bidar around 1470 and wrote one of the earliest European descriptions of the Bahmani court and its people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The merchant <b>Afanasy Nikitin</b> set out from Tver for India in the 1460s.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-tver">Grand Principality of Tver</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tver, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.86,35.92&amp;year=1475" aria-label="Tver on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Afanasy Nikitin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5276 5256h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5276 5256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.86,35.92&amp;year=1475">What was here in 1475? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
